import{t as e}from"./utils-COoNfFoj.js";var t={slug:`shervin-shares`,influencerName:`Shervin Shares`,influencerHandle:`@ShervinShares`,influencerAvatarUrl:`${e}/shervin-shares-avatar.jpg`,heroDescription:`I cramped up due to the heat at the London Marathon, went out too fast on the hills at the New York City Marathon, and got hit by the heat again at the Berlin Marathon. I really want a tool that helps me run at the proper pace based on weather and elevation so I can finally set a new PR, especially after missing it in three marathons back-to-back.`,assetTitle:`Marathon Race Planner`,screenshotUrl:`${e}/shervin-shares.png`,remixUrl:`https://www.perplexity.ai/computer/a/marathon-race-planner-REkZLRW7SfmdZLFoDdEFkA`,promptTitle:`Try Shervin's prompt`,promptText:`Build an interactive race conditions dashboard for all six World Marathon Majors: Tokyo, Boston, London, Berlin, Chicago, and New York City. Use the three attached screenshots as visual inspiration for the colors, fonts, layout, and overall style.

Include a marathon selector dropdown at the top. When I change the marathon, instantly update everything: the course map with mile markers and elevation profile, along with a conditions panel showing temperature, humidity, dew point (calculated automatically from temperature and humidity), wind speed, wind direction, and elevation gain for that specific course.

Pull the live weather forecast automatically for the race day and location using Open-Meteo. Display a visual risk rating for race-day conditions: Ideal, Moderate, Challenging, or Severe. Flag any conditions that are likely to slow my pace or increase my heart rate.

Keep all units in Fahrenheit, miles, and mph. Make the experience clean, visual, and responsive, with instant updates whenever I switch marathons. Add a mile-by-mile race pace calculator that dynamically updates based on the conditions shown in the race conditions dashboard.

Connect to my Strava account and pull in my past runs. For each run, retrieve the historical weather from Open-Meteo for the corresponding date and location.

Research the top 10 marathon energy gels ranked by carbohydrate content, along with the top 10 electrolyte and salt products commonly used by endurance runners. For each product, include carbohydrates per serving, sodium per serving, and caffeine content, if applicable. Then research sports science studies and recommendations for carbohydrate intake, sodium intake, and water intake during a marathon based on body weight, pace, temperature, and humidity.

Design a personalized pre-race nutrition plan for the selected World Marathon Major based on my body weight, goal finish time, and race date.

Search my Gmail inbox for all emails related to the selected World Marathon Major. Look for hotel confirmations, flight itineraries, race registration details, bib pickup instructions, expo information, race rules, and any race-week schedule details.

Extract the key details and display them as clean, easy-to-read summary cards.`};export{t as default};
